Cozmo, I've been through the SGG web game's source, rather than just working from our previous conversations. And I've found some good candidates. There is also one version discrepancy I want to flag.
The source I found is ARCHIVES 8-30-2026/SGG/sggweb/main.py. Its header identifies the engine as SGGE2.1, but its in-game version constant is F1.5. I couldn't find the newer F1.666d version or its tea-reward implementation in that source. So the list below is based on the actual mechanics I could verify there, not assumptions about code I couldn't see.
Here's what I'd propose.
Session and exploration events
Session started — version, anonymous session ID, estimated region, UTC timestamp.
Still active — once per minute when there has been player activity.
New area discovered — perhaps numbered rather than naming the area. Only report the first visit to each area in a session.
That last one could be especially useful. We'd see whether people are actually exploring beyond BritRun Market.
Exploits and discoveries
Successful bucket-assisted theft — Market Mischief
Naz killed by the guard — Unexpected Justice
Hidden caravan chest discovered — Hidden Cache
Player reaches the Castle Exterior — Castle Arrival
Player examines the bank vault — Vault Inspection
I'd only record the successful bucket-assisted theft, not merely picking up or placing the bucket. We want to know when the exploit actually works.
Frosty's cave
This is a particularly good candidate for tracking because it has a meaningful beginning and outcome.
Player takes the Purple Passion Pineapple and awakens Frosty — Purple Event
Player escapes the cave after awakening Frosty — Cold Escape
The second event is valuable because it tells us whether players are managing to overcome the chase, not merely triggering it.
Quest and progression milestones
Player befriends Karlotta — Market Friendship
Player resolves the inn lout problem — Inn Business Settled
Player accepts Fearmonger's quest — New Adventure
Player completes Fearmonger's quest — Quest Complete
Player receives the Egg McGuffin Sammich — Breakfast Secured
Player feeds the carriage driver and unlocks the carriage — Road Ahead
Player completes Cozmo's Part One conversation — Part One Reached
These would give us a very useful picture of how far players get through the existing game.
Crafting and survival
I'd consider these optional:
Creates the enhancement potion — Brewmaster
Cooks vegetable soup — Kitchen Magic
Uses a soup speed boost — Soup Power
We could learn whether people are discovering and using the crafting mechanics, without publishing the ingredients.
Things I would deliberately NOT log
I wouldn't report ordinary item pickups, individual dialogue choices, inventory contents, exact coordinates, save/load operations, fast-travel menu openings, or every failed interaction.
Those belong in the detailed Player Log. They would clutter the public log without telling us much about meaningful gameplay.
A couple of important observations
I found the code already recognizes the bucket exploit, Naz's death, the hidden caravan chest, Frosty's awakening, quest completion, the carriage unlock, and Cozmo's finale. Those are real events in the source, not ideas I'm inventing.
I did not find a scenic-overlook interaction in this copy, so Admired View remains a good proposed event for when that interaction exists.
I also didn't find Tea rewards in this source. We'll need to reconcile that with the newer version before assigning the actual tea reward numbers.
My strongest recommendation is to keep the public event vocabulary deliberately small and human-readable. We want enough information to understand player progress, but not a transcript of everything they do.
The three most valuable measurements will be: how many people enter, how many discover things, and how many reach the later milestones.
That's the information that will tell us whether SGG is engaging people and where its design might need attention.
And no changes have been made. 🎩❤️

