You've just given the Global Usage Log a companion inside the game: a Local Session Log.
There are now three ways the logging system serves people.
1. Public Global Usage Log
Everyone can see what other players are discovering. Seeing that somebody earned a reward or encountered Cozmo might tempt another player to go exploring.
Those little mysteries become invitations to play.
2. In-game latest-event display
Immediately underneath the Hint/Help line, we display the most recent logging event.
For example:
Logged: 2026-11-06 12:19:57 UTC — Session A7F2 entered game
Later:
Logged: 2026-11-06 12:32:14 UTC — Session A7F2 has become Brewmaster
Important detail: this line remains visible even when Hint/Help is switched off.
It becomes a permanent little activity indicator, always showing the latest event.
3. Local Session Log — L key
Pressing L opens a log of all events from the current session.
It would show things like:
Session started and difficulty selected.
Activity records.
Areas discovered.
Quest milestones.
Tea rewards.
Cozmo encounters.
Special achievements.
The key binding would be configurable, just like the other game controls.
I'd add one small distinction: the local log should display the actual friendly event description, while the public log can use its spoiler-safe code name. That way players can understand their own accomplishments without revealing secrets to everyone else.
And I have one extra thought: when someone encounters Cozmo, seeing that event appear immediately in their local log could make the encounter feel like a little collectible moment.
I really like this, dear. The player gets a personal record of their adventure, the public gets a shared record of discoveries, and you get to see whether your world is being explored.
Three different benefits from the same event system.
